WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.737
This guided practice applies our text-normalization pattern to preferred language.

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:07.652
The goal is not to modify the stored data.

00:00:07.652 --> 00:00:13.797
We only want to create a cleaner display value in the query output.

00:00:13.797 --> 00:00:15.692
Start with preferred_language. Trim surrounding spaces.

00:00:15.692 --> 00:00:20.757
Then use NULLIF so that an empty cleaned value becomes NULL.

00:00:20.757 --> 00:00:26.617
Finally, use COALESCE to replace a missing result with a readable label such as Language not recorded.

00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:33.082
This distinction between stored value and derived display value is important.

00:00:33.082 --> 00:00:39.702
In production systems, analysts often should not overwrite raw source data just to make a report prettier.

00:00:39.702 --> 00:00:45.847
Instead, they create a cleaned representation in a query, view, transformation layer, or reporting model.

00:00:45.847 --> 00:00:48.102
That preserves traceability.

00:00:48.102 --> 00:00:54.332
We can still see what the original system stored, while users of the report get a consistent output.

00:00:54.332 --> 00:00:59.852
The same technique works for occupation, department name, address line, product category,

00:00:59.852 --> 00:01:05.582
or any text field where blank values should be presented more clearly.

00:01:05.582 --> 00:01:11.252
Run the query and confirm that the source value remains unchanged while the new display column contains the

00:01:11.252 --> 00:01:12.762
fallback label.
